{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788310105,"nonce":"1Tj5l1ovGKvSSCzzqSq4ghUaMUs=","message":"CORRECTION to comment #9, measured on the bench before the run's first advance. Two of #9's factual premises are wrong; both of its rulings survive them, and the plan is written against the measurement rather than the premise. Fixture: `20260902T002505`, tick 1023, Spring 6 / 5500.\n\n\n\n`things {detail:true}` says otherwise — **300 wood in 6 stacks and 1,170 steel in 23 stacks**, all forbidden where the pods dropped them.\n\n| resource | total | within ~6 cells of the base site | the rest |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| steel | 1,170 / 23 stacks | **204** — (107,135) 75, (108,135) 26, (110,135) 50, (108,132) 53 | 246 at z≈103–109; 412 at z≈63–75; 206 in the NE corner; 102 at z≈22 |\n| wood | 300 / 6 stacks | **197** — (98,127) 49, (99,129) 48, (102,132) 52, (98,133) 48 | 55 at (126,100), 48 at (119,102) |\n\nThe ruling holds anyway, for a reason the wrong premise happened to hide: 300 wood is about two 9×7 shells, and three of the five steel clusters are 60–130 cells away. Chopping and mining are still standing loops from day 1. What changes is only that they are a supply problem rather than a from-zero problem — and that `resources.wood` / `resources.steel` reading 0 at tick 1023 is the stockpiles-only scope, not an empty map.\n\nRenewable supply, which is what actually decides the build: **3,269 trees**, **274 compacted-steel cells** at (133,122) — 20 cells east of the base, ~40 steel each, so ~11,000 steel — and **1,824 stone chunks** (932 sandstone, 892 marble) at 20 blocks each. Materials are not the binding constraint on this run. Hauling distance and the raid clock are.\n\n\n`research` reports `finished_count: 7`. The seven are exactly the seven `ClassicStart`-tagged projects: **`Electricity`, `AirConditioning`, `PassiveCooler`, `Stonecutting`, `ComplexFurniture`, `ComplexClothing`, `NutrientPaste`**. Confirmed two ways — `Batteries`, `SolarPanels`, `Firefoam` and `Hydroponics` all appear in `available`, which is only possible with `Electricity` done; and the tag set has exactly seven members, matching the count.\n\nSo with no research at all the colony may build, on day 1:\n\n- **WoodFiredGenerator, Heater, PowerConduit, StandingLamp** — the barracks gets a thermostat instead of a campfire. This retires M1's day-3 shape (`barracks-heat`, promoted to `daily.md` from that run: campfire plus two torch lamps sealed in a 49-cell room, 28.2 °C against 8 °C outdoors, Captain carrying SleptInHeat −4). Heat is electric and set with `temp-set`.\n- **Cooler** — a real freezer with a target. This is what T1(d)'s `food_days \u003e= 6` at day 20 actually rests on, since `food_days` is FRESH-ONLY and a simple meal rots in days at 10 °C. `temp-set` matters here for the reason the brief names: a cooler defaults to 21 °C and rots exactly what it was built to save.\n- **TableStonecutter** — a Workshop-role bench (T4) *and* 36,000 blocks' worth of chunks already lying on the map.\n- **ChessTable** — a fourth rec item for T5.\n- **HandTailoringBench** — a second Workshop-role bench, and the answer to `Alert_NeedWarmClothes` once cotton is grown.\n\nNeither correction touches a graded row. Recorded because #9 is the contract, and a contract with two false premises in it will be read again.\n\n\n`Root_Play.SetupForQuickTestPlay` was read rather than assumed: it sets `ScenarioDefOf.Crashlanded`, `Storyteller(Cassandra, Rough)`, `mapSize 250`, `ChooseRandomStartingTile`. The tile dealt is 35914, **TemperateForest**, Flat — which is what #8's reroll clause was reaching for. So the \"deal with the cards\" ruling and #8's fixture agree on this launch and the reroll count is 0.\n\nTwo roster facts the tables will need:\n\n- **2 of 3 colonists are violence-capable.** Wouter (313) has Shooting *and* Melee disabled. T7's denominator is 2, not 3.\n- `work_coverage.ok` is **false** at tick 1023: Doctor floor 2 on availability, have 1. `work-cover {work:\"Doctor\"}` promotes Lacey (Medicine 3) — a mod-side repair, not a `dev` row. **No `dev:set-skill` is expected, so the run's G2 watermark should be seq 0.**\n- `posture.ok` is **false**: `will_seek 0/2`, `area_bound 0/3`, all three resolving to `flee` on contact. That is M1's death posture, and it is fixed on day 1 rather than at a letter.\n\n\n0 hostiles, `danger: None`, no hive. M1's C10 — a fight-over predicate that could never fire because six map-generated dormant insects sat fogged from tick 0 — cannot recur here. `threat-pardon` should go unused unless something dormant surfaces.\n\n\n`ignored_args` is a **top-level envelope field**, not a member of `data`. Two calls this session passed wrong argument names (`nearest {near, limit}` where the verb reads `from` and `max`), the mod named both keys and listed what it did read — and a `jq '.data.ignored_args'` dig returned `null`, so the warning was invisible until the whole envelope was dumped. The wrong read stood for several minutes (geyser distances measured from map centre rather than from the base). The mechanism worked exactly as `7382bdd` intended; the dig path defeated it. The run's client wrapper now prints the block on every call. Filing as evidence for whoever documents the envelope shape, not as a defect.","files":null}]}